# Fixes applied: Supertraining Chapter 7 lead-in and 7.1 (Ch7a), 10 entries

Fixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch7a_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 7 FIX rows, 7 numbered fixes, 5 missing ideas).
Source checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 175, 335, 336 and 404-414 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f7a_text.txt`). I rendered PDF 175, 335, 336 and 404-411 and 414 to `staging/_renders/f7a_p<N>.png` and opened every page I quote from: 175 (3.4.2 Western and Russian after-effect studies), 335-336 (6.6.2 recording convention), 404 (five knowledge sources), 405 (superslow, active stretching, connective tissue, cyclical work), 406 (Fig 7.2 and "optimal recovery intervals between sets"), 407 (eccentrics and cheating warnings; no warning for forced reps), 408 (regimes, "best known", "popularly used", plateau "commonly used"), 409 (pyramids, interval training, 5RM minimum, 4-6RM, 10-20% strip), 410 (intensive clustering, segmental, hybrid), 411 (80³ over 3+5+2, contrast methods) and 414 (concluding remarks). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.
Scope: the 10 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt`. I edited the 7 FIX entries plus the GO chart entry (missing ideas). No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (10 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 7b or 6d file, or the vault. Edits were made with an exact-match replacement script (`staging/_tmp/f7a_apply.py`; each replacement asserted to match once).

| file | what changed |
|---|---|
| framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md | **Fix 1.** (1) "Why `contested`" now opens "The rating applies only to the rest between near-maximal singles; the load-band definitions are not in dispute." (2) "Both sentences describe" is now "As worded, both sentences describe", followed by: PDF p. 406 calls the 1-5 minutes "optimal recovery intervals between sets", and the PDF p. 409 sentence opens the interval section that leads straight into cluster training (10-20 s within an extended set), "so the two passages may describe different methods, sets of singles against clustered singles. The book does not say which, and does not reconcile them." (3) When NOT: "the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods" is now "the authors give injury warnings for eccentrics and cheating (PDF p. 407); forced repetitions sit in the same supramaximal group" (PDF 407 image: warnings for eccentrics and cheating only). Beyond the review, for consistency: When NOT's "two incompatible values" is now "two values that may describe different methods". `contested` kept. |
| framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md | **Fix 2.** `confidence: untested` is now `supported`. "Split routines and zone training suit athletes ..." now starts "Inference:". |
| framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md | **Fix 3.** `confidence: untested` is now `supported`. When to apply: "Inference: step sets can find a working load for a fixed repetition target." When NOT: "Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases (PDF p. 408). Inference: not for a peaking block." The entry keeps the authors' "Pyramiding is rarely used by serious athletes today" caveat; `supported` rates the structures as established practice, and the caveat is the authors' own view on which structure serious athletes prefer. |
| framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md | **Fix 4.** "Why `contested`" now leads with the direct text: the same paragraph sets the minimum at "one's 5RM" (PDF p. 409) but prescribes extensive clustering "with one's 4-6RM" (a 6RM is lighter than a 5RM) and a stripping form that cuts the load "by 10-20%" after each set (PDF pp. 409-410), "both of which take the load below that minimum"; intensive clustering then gives "75-90% of 1RM" (PDF p. 410). The pyramid-based Inference is kept after it. When NOT now reads "without choosing among its load statements (the 5RM minimum against the 4-6RM, the 10-20% strip and 75-90% of 1RM)". Beyond the review, for consistency with fix 1: the "second internal conflict" paragraph now adds "The two passages may describe different methods (sets of singles against clustered singles), and the book does not say which". `contested` kept. |
| framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md | **Fix 5.** The "Unclear as printed ... may be a set count" note is replaced: "By the 6.6.2 convention (PDF pp. 335-336 / printed pp. 324-325, checked against the page images, where "60 x 10, 70 x 8, 80 x 6, 80 x 6" is also written "10(60)¹, 8(70)¹, 6(80)²", so "6(80)²" means two sets of 6 at 80 kg) the superscript is the number of sets, so 80³ over 3+5+2 means three sets of the 10-repetition hybrid; the text's "for a set of 3 repetitions" conflicts with its own convention." When to apply now opens with the authors' use, "When a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use, PDF p. 410)", and the time/space clause follows as "Inference: also when ...". The Example now says "written 80³ (three sets by the 6.6.2 convention)". `untested` kept. |
| heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md | **Fix 6.** "The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled" is now "The random variant from the start of a block, to limit early accommodation (the authors' stated purpose, PDF p. 410)" (PDF 410 image: "to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime"). `untested` kept. |
| framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md | **Fix 7.** (1) "Why `contested`" now opens "The rating applies to the heavy-light set." "The heavy-light set and load release rest on ..." is now "The heavy-light set rests on exactly the effect these studies disagree about", followed by "Load release and pneumatics unload within a single repetition; the cited studies did not test that, so the conflict does not reach them directly." (2) The Russian findings on PDF 175 are quoted verbatim with their citation lists: "Dynamic work with heavy weights (i.e. high tension with relatively few repetitions) also elicits a positive after-effect in the central nervous system (CNS), which produces a general toning influence on the motor apparatus and an improvement in speed and strength (Portnov, 1955; Ratov, 1957; Diachkov, 1961; Muravov & Tkachev, 1964; Letunov, 1965)" and "intense preliminary strength tension improves results in jumping exercises (Diachkov, 1958; 1961; Verkhoshansky, 1961; Tatian, 1964; Tkachev, 1967), in shotputting (Fritsch, 1961; Ivanova, 1964; Markov, 1966), and in rowing (Ermishkin & Vozniak, 1965; Chuprun, 1966)". When NOT now reads "Do not expect a reliable acute boost from the heavy-light set: the Western studies the book cites are split (two found a faster unloaded movement, others found none), and the Russian studies it cites found one (PDF p. 175)." `contested` kept. |
| framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md | **Missing ideas 1-5** (GO entry). New paragraph "Sources and scope of the catalogue": the five sources (PDF 404), "Only in recent years, has science begun to investigate seriously ..." (PDF 404), and the concluding "rapid reference for the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes" plus the magazine-methods exclusion (PDF 414), with "Inference: most of the 7.1 catalogue rests on practice in the strength sports, not on studies cited on these pages." New block "Notes beside Fig 7.1" (PDF 405) with four quoted bullets: superslow, active stretching, connective tissue first (Chapter 1 gives the basis and Chapter 6 the rule, both linked), low-intensity cyclical work. When NOT adds "Do not read the catalogue as tested: the authors say science has only "in recent years" begun to examine Iron Game practice (PDF p. 404)." Related adds five links (below). `untested` kept. |

## Confidence rule, all 10 entries

Rule applied (coordinator decision): established, parameterised practice that the authors present with loads, reps, rest or other parameters is `supported`; anecdotes, novelties the authors discount, tip lists and orderings with no evidence are `untested`; `contested` stays where a same-question conflict is recorded. The book's own basis for the rule is the 7.1 concluding remark (PDF 414): the catalogue covers "the major methods which are most frequently used in the training of high-level athletes".

| entry | before | after | reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| Figs 7.1 and 7.2 chart | untested | untested | A taxonomy. Its one empirical claim, the "increasing importance of nervous system training" arrow, has no evidence (PDF 405). |
| Maximal / supramaximal / circa-max / submax | contested | contested | Same-question conflict on rest between singles, now scoped to that rest figure only. |
| Three bodybuilding regimes | untested | **supported** | Fix 2. Sets, reps, load and rest given; "one of the best known elementary bodybuilding methods", add-ons "popularly used" (PDF 408). Same footing as Table 1.1. |
| Set structures | untested | **supported** | Fix 3. Parameterised; plateau "commonly used" (PDF 408), pyramid values "derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9" (PDF 409), stripping "commonly used" (PDF 408). |
| Interval training and cluster sets | contested | contested | Same-paragraph conflict on cluster load (fix 4). |
| Hybrid repetitions and sets | untested | untested | Repetition counts but no load or rest guidance; the examples "may be devised by the inventive coach" (PDF 411). The "commonly used" on PDF 411 refers to the Ch 8.4 list, not atomized here. |
| Progressive and random segmental | untested | untested | Twenty-Ones is "A popular form" with repetition counts (PDF 410), but no load or rest is given; the random variant is a named novelty ("For want of a better term"); Rigert is a single anecdote with no outcome. The rule does not clearly require a change. |
| Contrast methods | contested | contested | The heavy-light set is parameterised (20%, 10-20 s, 3-5 reps), but the recorded conflict on PDF 175 outranks that. |
| Miscellaneous bodybuilding sets | untested | untested | The authors call these "of minimal significance" for sport (PDF 412). |
| 27 sticking-point strategies | untested | untested | A tip list ("Among some of the methods are the following", PDF 413), no basis for most items. |

